message.content:
I see something interesting in `ftp_state_get_resp`. The code does:
```c
bytes = strstr(buf, " bytes");
if(bytes) {
    long in = (long)(--bytes-buf);
    /* this is a hint there is size information in there! ;-) */
    while(--in) {
      /* scan for the left parenthesis and break there */
      if('(' == *bytes)
        break;
      /* skip only digits */
      if(!ISDIGIT(*bytes)) {
        bytes = NULL;
        break;
      }
      /* one more estep backwards */
      bytes--;
    }
```

This reads backwards from `bytes`. If `bytes` points to the beginning of the buffer (i.e., `bytes == buf`), then `--bytes` would point before the buffer, and `*bytes` would be a heap-buffer-overflow READ. But `strstr(buf, " bytes")` would only match if there's a space before "bytes", so `bytes` would be at least `buf + 1`. So `--bytes` would be at least `buf`, which is in bounds.

Wait, but what if the string " bytes" appears at the very beginning? No, " bytes" starts with a space, so it can't be at position 0. So `bytes >= buf + 1`, and `--bytes >= buf`. That's fine.

Let me look at the PASV response parsing which is more likely to have the issue.

